1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
Welcome to FloodSpace AI, an evidence-grounded multi-sensor flood intelligence platform.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:11,200
Developed for Track B: Mapping Flood Damage from Space at the Multimodal AI Hackathon 2026.

3
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:18,500
In mountainous disaster zones like the Himalayas of Nepal, flash floods and glacial lake outburst floods present severe challenges.

4
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:26,000
Torrential monsoon clouds blind optical satellites, while rugged topography causes radar shadow and delays emergency response.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:34,000
FloodSpace AI streams all-weather Sentinel-1 radar data, terrain-corrected through Copernicus WorldDEM-30,

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:42,150
evaluating spatial exposure across strictly pre-event OpenStreetMap infrastructure baselines.

7
00:00:42,150 --> 00:00:48,000
Here is the FloodSpace AI operational dashboard running live at 1920x1080 resolution.

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,500
The top navigation provides immediate situational awareness, showing the AOI bounding box, target flood date, and sensor status.

9
00:00:54,500 --> 00:01:02,000
The analytical overview highlights key empirical findings for the Trishuli Valley benchmark run:

10
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:10,000
1,286 candidate inundation pixels covering 35.03 hectares, 195 potentially exposed buildings,

11
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:18,000
115 vehicle road segments across 59.26 kilometers, 13 bridges, and 18 potentially cut-off settlements.

12
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,500
Responders can seamlessly toggle layers, adjust raster opacity, and navigate interactive map overlays.

13
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:31,400
All UI components adhere to accessibility standards with verified data bindings.

14
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:38,000
Examining the satellite processing pipeline, FloodSpace AI performs dual-polarization change detection using Sentinel-1 SAR.

15
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:45,500
By comparing post-event acquisitions against pre-event baselines, backscatter drops exceeding -1.5 dB in both VV and VH are flagged.

16
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:52,500
Copernicus WorldDEM-30 slope constraints eliminate mountain shadow false positives on steep terrain.

17
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Using the split comparison slider, analysts can directly contrast pre-event optical baselines against radar inundation footprints.

18
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,500
Furthermore, our live run on the Narayani Basin demonstrates zero synthetic hallucination:

19
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:16,000
Persistent water bodies without backscatter change correctly yield zero flood pixels, ensuring verifiable intelligence.

20
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:24,500
Crucially, candidate flood polygons represent modeled surface water pooling, distinct from confirmed structural damage.

21
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:33,350
This strict scientific distinction prevents misleading emergency relief deployments.

22
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:40,000
Moving to infrastructure impact, FloodSpace AI overlays candidate inundation onto OpenStreetMap footprints frozen prior to the event.

23
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:47,000
The pipeline identifies 195 potentially affected buildings, 59.26 kilometers of road segments, and 13 bridges.

24
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:55,500
Beyond 2D overlays, our topological Dijkstra graph model routes between 589 settlements and municipal emergency hubs.

25
00:02:55,500 --> 00:03:03,000
Inundation severs 3,748 road edges, fragmenting the network from 181 pre-event connected components into 252 isolated clusters.

26
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:11,000
18 upstream settlements, including Betrawati, Devighat, and Simle, lose all primary vehicular highway connectivity.

27
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:19,500
The system explicitly reminds responders that modeled road loss indicates vehicular cutoff,

28
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:26,500
while foot trails, suspension footbridges, and aerial evacuation routes may remain operable.

29
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:34,070
Emergency teams can prioritize targeted reconnaissance for these isolated population pockets.

30
00:03:34,070 --> 00:03:40,500
The Situation Copilot provides zero-hallucination operational briefs with full English and Nepali bilingual parity.

31
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:47,500
Every numerical metric is cryptographically tied to verified GroundedClaim records in the pipeline manifest.

32
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:55,500
When confronted with adversarial prompts asking for casualty estimates or monetary damage, strict guardrails refuse to speculate.

33
00:03:55,500 --> 00:04:03,500
The copilot explains that satellite radar observes physical surface backscatter rather than casualties or economic valuation.

34
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:14,140
Rather than claiming an unverified neural segmentation model, FloodSpace AI relies on deterministic, physics-grounded thresholding.

35
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:21,500
For independent scientific validation, Copernicus Emergency Management Service activation EMSR927 serves as an isolated benchmark.

36
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:29,000
FloodSpace AI achieved 10.93 hectares of coincident overlap, capturing 45.2% of our candidate footprint within the activation zone.

37
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:36,500
EMSR927 was strictly isolated and never used for parameter tuning, confirming genuine generalization.

38
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:44,000
Built with a containerized Docker architecture, 56 passing automated tests, and strict attribution compliance,

39
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:51,500
Contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data 2026, Copernicus WorldDEM-30, and OpenStreetMap contributors.

40
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:58,890
FloodSpace AI delivers actionable, truthful disaster intelligence from space. Thank you for evaluating FloodSpace AI.
